.TH GXWI-TASK-MANAGER 1 2026-10-19 "Peios gxwi-task-manager 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
gxwi-task-manager \- see what is running, and who is signed in, on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-task-manager
.SH DESCRIPTION
Task Manager shows running processes and sessions in four views.
Processes lists every process, with its PID, who it runs as, and its share of the processor and memory.
Group by arranges them by service or job, or by person, and Find narrows them.
The process picked is shown in full beside it: what started it, its command, its service or job, its session, its integrity, its protection and its mitigations.
Performance graphs the last two minutes of the processors, memory, each disk and each network interface.
Jobs lists the jobs peinit is running.
Signed in lists each logon session: who, how, since when, and its processes.
.PP
It reads /proc as the person looking, and asks peinit which services and jobs there are and authd who each SID is, with no more authority than they have.
What they cannot see is left out or marked, and the window says how much and why.
As shipped, who a process runs as and its command are visible to that person and to Administrators.
A protected process is closed to everyone, Administrators included, but is still listed.
The full list of sessions is the kernel's, which only Administrators can read; anyone else sees the sessions their own processes are in.
.PP
End process asks first, then asks the process to end, holding it by a pidfd so that another given the same PID cannot be ended by mistake; one still there after five seconds is offered End it now.
Stop service and Stop job ask peinit to stop what the process belongs to.
Sign out asks authd to end a session and every process in it.
Each is offered only where the person can: End process where the process's own descriptor lets them, Stop where peinit says they can stop it, and Sign out for their own sessions and, as shipped, anyone's to Administrators.
Where they cannot, the window says why.
.PP
Everything is read again every two seconds; F5, or Refresh, reads it at once.
Nothing is kept once the window closes.
What is defined, rather than what is running, is Services Manager's.
.SH SECURITY
Process, peinit, and session operations use the requester's access.
A protected process can be visible in the list without readable details or termination access.
A retained process descriptor protects termination against process-ID reuse.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The application session ended normally.
.TP
.B 1
A connection, initialization, or application error prevented normal operation.
.TP
.B 64
Invalid command arguments.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GXWI_DISPLAY
The display connection supplied by the desktop session.
Launch through the desktop so inherited session descriptors and identity are available.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-task-manager.toml
Its application declaration, listed in a launcher as Task Manager.
.TP
.I /proc
The processes, and the machine's processor, memory, disk and network figures.
.SH SEE ALSO
Task Manager, and The Process Security Block, at learn.peios.org.
